<article class="hd-anchor"><header></header></article>
<div class="shell">
	<nav class="hd-trail" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">PCNMobile</a> / <span>Articles</span></nav>	<div class="hd-split">
		<div class="hd-col">
			<article id="post-750519" class="hd-article post-750519 post type-post status-publish format-standard hentry category-blog tag-ai-models tag-linux tag-macos tag-ollama tag-storage tag-windows mv-content-wrapper">
				<header>
					<p class="hd-kicker">Article</p>
					<h1>How to Change Ollama’s Model Storage Location</h1>
					<p class="hd-byline">
						<span>By <a href="https://pcnmobile.com/author/pcnmobile/">PCNMobile Team</a></span>
						<time datetime="2026-10-04T14:52:01+05:30">4 Oct 2026</time>
												<span>3 min read</span>
					</p>
				</header>
								<div class="entry-content article-content">
					<p>Set the <code>OLLAMA_MODELS</code> environment variable to the full path of the directory where you want Ollama to store models. The variable must be available to the Ollama server process—not just an unrelated terminal—and changing it does not, by itself, move models already downloaded.</p>
<h2>Where Ollama stores models by default</h2>
<p>Ollama uses <code>OLLAMA_MODELS</code> when it is set. Otherwise, it falls back to a <code>models</code> directory beneath the running process user’s <code>.ollama</code> directory. The documented standard paths are:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Platform</th>
<th>Documented default model directory</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>macOS</td>
<td><code>~/.ollama/models</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Linux</td>
<td><code>/usr/share/ollama/.ollama/models</code> for the standard installation described in the FAQ</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Windows</td>
<td><code>C:Users%username%.ollamamodels</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The effective fallback depends on the account running Ollama. On Linux, a system service can run as the <code>ollama</code> account, whose home directory may differ from the administrator’s. Ollama’s <a href="https://github.com/ollama/ollama/blob/main/envconfig/config.go">environment configuration</a> describes the general fallback as the process user’s <code>$HOME/.ollama/models</code>.</p>
<h2>Choose a destination with enough space</h2>
<p>Choose a full path on a volume with sufficient free space. Ollama’s <a href="https://github.com/ollama/ollama/blob/main/docs/windows.md">Windows installation guide</a> says model storage can require “tens to hundreds of GB.” A larger existing drive can be used as the destination; an external drive is not inherently required.</p>
<h2>Change the model location on Windows</h2>
<ol>
<li>Open the Windows account’s Environment Variables settings. Create a user variable named <code>OLLAMA_MODELS</code>, or edit it if it already exists.</li>
<li>Set its value to the complete destination path, for example <code>D:Ollamamodels</code>. Create the destination folder if needed.</li>
<li>Click <strong>Apply</strong> and <strong>OK</strong> to save the change.</li>
<li>Quit Ollama from the tray, then launch it again from Start. If you start Ollama from a terminal, open a new terminal after saving the variable.</li>
</ol>
<p>Ollama inherits environment variables when it starts, so an already-running app will not pick up a change made afterward. The restart guidance is in the project’s <a href="https://github.com/ollama/ollama/blob/main/docs/windows.md">Windows documentation</a>.</p>
<h2>Change the location on Linux with systemd</h2>
<p>For the standard Linux installation, set the variable in the service environment. Setting it only in your interactive shell will not configure a separately running system service.</p>
<ol>
<li>Run <code>sudo systemctl edit ollama</code> to open a service drop-in editor.</li>
<li>Add this configuration, replacing the example path with your destination:<br /><code>[Service]<br />Environment="OLLAMA_MODELS=/mnt/storage/ollama/models"</code></li>
<li>Save and close the editor, then reload systemd and restart Ollama:<br /><code>sudo systemctl daemon-reload<br />sudo systemctl restart ollama</code></li>
<li>Ensure the destination exists and that the <code>ollama</code> service account can read and write to it. Without the required access, model downloads may fail.</li>
</ol>
<p>Ollama’s <a href="https://github.com/ollama/ollama/blob/main/docs/faq.md">FAQ</a> covers service environment configuration and access requirements; its <a href="https://github.com/ollama/ollama/blob/main/docs/linux.md">Linux guide</a> describes systemd customization. If you run <code>ollama serve</code> manually instead, set <code>OLLAMA_MODELS</code> in the environment used to start that server process.</p>
<!-- YM_DONATION_SCOPE_KOFI_20260610A --><a class="ym-donation-kofi" href="https://ko-fi.com/yorkermedia" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow" aria-label="Support this site"><span class="ym-donation-kofi__body"><span class="ym-donation-kofi__eyebrow">Independent reader support</span><span class="ym-donation-kofi__text">Your contribution helps us test, update, and keep practical guides available for everyone.</span></span><span class="ym-donation-kofi__button">Support on Ko-Fi</span></a><h2>Change the location on macOS</h2>
<p>Set <code>OLLAMA_MODELS</code> in the environment visible to the Ollama application or server, then restart the application. The official <a href="https://github.com/ollama/ollama/blob/main/docs/faq.md">FAQ</a> points macOS users to its environment-variable guidance, which uses <code>launchctl setenv</code> followed by an application restart. Confirm that the variable is available to the process that serves Ollama; a value set only in a separate shell may not reach an app launched independently.</p>
<h2>What happens to models already downloaded?</h2>
<p>Setting a new directory chooses where Ollama will look for model storage under the new configuration; the official instructions cited here do not say that changing the variable automatically moves existing files. Do not delete the old model directory on the assumption that it has been migrated. Keep it until Ollama can see the models at the new location, and consult current platform-specific migration guidance if you need exact file-transfer steps.</p>
<h2>Check the setting if it does not take effect</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Confirm the server can see the variable.</strong> Restart Ollama after changing the environment. A service or app launched separately does not necessarily inherit variables set in your current shell.</li>
<li><strong>Check the path.</strong> Make sure the destination exists and the configured value is the intended full path.</li>
<li><strong>Check permissions on Linux.</strong> The <code>ollama</code> service account needs read and write access to the destination.</li>
<li><strong>Check available space.</strong> Model files can take substantial room; confirm the chosen volume has enough free capacity.</li>
</ul>
<p>For container deployments, set <code>OLLAMA_MODELS</code> in the environment of the container running the server, and ensure the chosen directory is mounted there and writable. The cited Ollama pages do not provide a complete container-specific volume migration procedure, so use the instructions for your deployment before changing mounts or moving existing data.</p>
<aside class="ymel ymel--post_end" data-ymel-src="post_end" aria-label="Newsletter sign-up"><div class="ymel-brand"><img src="https://pcnmobile.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/pn-site-icon-512-150x150.png" alt="" width="20" height="20"  decoding="async"><span>PCnMobile newsletter</span></div><div class="ymel-t">More Windows guides like this, every week</div><div class="ymel-d">One short e-mail a week. Free, no spam &mdash; unsubscribe in one click.</div><form class="ymel-f" method="post" action="https://pcnmobile.com/wp-json/ym/v1/subscribe" novalidate><label class="ymel-sr" for="ymel-e-post_end">E-mail address</label><input class="ymel-i" id="ymel-e-post_end" type="email" name="email" autocomplete="email" inputmode="email" placeholder="you@example.com" required maxlength="190"><input type="text" name="website" class="ymel-hp" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" aria-hidden="true"><input type="hidden" name="ts" value="1792903368.372dea833cdc840bba93"><input type="hidden" name="source" value="post_end"><button class="ymel-b" type="submit">Join free</button></form><div class="ymel-m" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div><div class="ymel-p">Your address stays with us &mdash; <a href="https://pcnmobile.com/privacy-policy/">privacy</a>.</div></aside>				</div>
